# Kept prompt states short of the prompt's end, so the next turn that branches off in its last tokens still resumes.
#
# A kept prompt state is a recurrent (KDA) state: a later prompt resumes from it only when it holds every one of its
# tokens. grid_point keeps a request's own state at the last grid point of its prompt (with TF_GLM_KDA_CHUNKED=1, the
# default, a 64-token grid), i.e. within 63 tokens of its end, often at the end itself. A next prompt that differs in
# those last tokens finds no usable state of its own and resumes at the conversation's previous kept state, or at 0: the
# generation prompt ends in tokens the client does not send back (GLM's <think> after <|assistant|>), a retried or
# edited last message, a client that re-renders the tail. Measured on four Sparks (TP=4, 64 kept states): 2 of 12
# follow-up turns of 17k-token conversations prefilled from 0; a 200k conversation's branching turn took 79 s instead
# of ~0.7 s.
#
# TF_GLM_KEEP_MARGIN=M (default 0: off, as before): the state is kept at floor((n - M) / grid) * grid, M to M + grid - 1
# tokens short of the end, still on the grid (the spill tier and _longest_kept are unchanged), when that lies past the
# resume point. Shared-prefix points stay short of it. Cost: every resumed turn prefills up to M + grid - 1 tokens again
# (~0.05 s at M=64), and an exact replay of a whole prompt prefills its tail instead of sampling the kept head row.
# Every rank checks the value with the other start-up settings. tools/keep_margin_check.py.

